lesson k-follow-the-arrows v=1 format=teach grade=0 unit=4 subject=coding levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Follow the arrows"
  goal "Move a robot on a grid by following arrow blocks, count its steps to a goal, and put three steps in order."

  look {
    scene 30x10 {
      row top at=canvas(1, 0) space=1 align=start {
        blocks bl code=["right 3"] words=false numbers=false
        stage s cols=7 col=1 who=bot code=["right 3"] flag=false upto=-1
      }
    }
    say """
      A block with an arrow tells the robot where to go, and the number tells it how many steps. This
      one says three steps to the right: one, two, three. A program is steps in order, and the
      robot does exactly what the blocks say.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked k.rabbit-hops n=2 level=medium
    }
    practice k.rabbit-hops count=2 seed=2
    practice k.which-way count=2 seed=3
    practice k.steps-to-flag count=2 seed=4 easy-count=1
    show k.teeth-in-order k=0 hard-k=1
    level medium hard {
      show k.teeth-in-order k=2
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show k.fix-the-number a=3 w=5 easy-a=2 easy-w=4 hard-a=4 hard-w=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show k.right-then-down a=2 b=1 easy-a=1 hard-a=3 hard-b=2
  }
  remember {
    say "Follow each block in order. Count every step."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Read each question aloud; the blocks are pictures, so the child reads the arrow and the dots.
    Be the robot first: stand on a square of floor and step exactly as the block says, which shows
    that the robot does only what it is told. Counting the starting square as a step is the usual
    slip. Off the page, give each other instructions to reach a toy across the room, one step at a
    time. England's early years goals do not name programming; the line quoted is Japan's.
    Japan, 幼稚園教育要領 (kindergarten guidelines), 環境, 内容 (7):
    "身近な物や遊具に興味をもってかかわり，考えたり，試したりして工夫して遊ぶ。" (Take an interest in
    familiar things and toys, and play inventively by thinking and trying things out.)
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level two hops are worked in the teacher's pen first, the arrows are right and down, the flag is one or two steps away, and the three-star robot goes one step down."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the hops are four or five, the arrows are left and up, and the three-star robot goes two steps down."
  }
}
